# End-to-end EMULATOR test for the Anycubic Kobra Go (HDSC HC32F460)
# in DETERMINISTIC TICK MODE. Boots klipper firmware under Renode
# against the kobra-go pin map,
# completes klippy identify + clocksync, brings up the full MCU config
# (4 plain step/dir steppers, 2 software-PWM heaters, fans, endstops,
# 2 thermistors), and reads both thermistors through the HC32 ADC.
#
# This is the HC32F460 per-printer test, the counterpart to
# hc32f460_smoke (boot only). Unlike the other per-printer tests
# (skr_v1_4, duet2_maestro, ...), the Kobra Go has no Trinamic drivers,
# so there is no TMC UART/SPI round-trip - the new infrastructure this
# exercises is the HC32 ADC:
#
#   - test/emulator/hc32f460_adc_stub.py serves M4_ADC1 @ 0x40040000.
#     klipper's src/hc32f460/adc.c kicks a conversion by writing STR=1,
#     polls ISR.EOCAF for completion, then reads the 16-bit DRn result
#     register (DR0 @ 0x50, channel index = (off-0x50)/2). The HC32
#     ADC register layout matches no upstream Renode model and the
#     hc32f460.repl maps no ADC, so the stub is the only thing at that
#     base. With a broken ADC the burst never completes, no
#     analog_in_state reaches klippy, and both heaters silently sit at
#     temp=0.0 with no shutdown - so the "temp=3" assertion below (both
#     thermistors read ~36 C) is the positive proof the ADC stub
#     delivered samples. The hotend reads through channel 13 (PC3, ATC
#     Semitec 104GT-2) and the bed through channel 11 (PC1, EPCOS 100K).
#
# The real Kobra Go is USB-CDC (CH340); the firmware here is the
# generic serial-mode hc32f460-serial-PA3PA2 build (USART1 host link on
# PA3/PA2, the same build hc32f460_smoke uses) and the test exercises
# the pin map, which is independent of the host link. The gated path is
# motion-free.
DICTIONARY hc32f460-serial-PA3PA2.dict
CONFIG anycubic_kobra_go.cfg
EMULATOR anycubic_kobra_go.fixture.json
REQUIRES_EMULATOR  # deterministic-time lockstep needs the real backend

# A few interactive gcode lines against real firmware: M105 reports the
# thermistor temperatures (the ADC read path end to end); M114 reports
# toolhead position. Neither drives a stepper.
M114
M105
# Dwell so the ADC's periodic query fires and both thermistors read a
# live value before the test exits. The Kobra Go has no TMC init to
# otherwise consume sim-time (the other per-printer tests' DUMP_TMC
# advances the clock), so without the dwell the temp reading can miss
# the stats-line window - same pattern as duet3_6xd.
G4 P2000
M105

# klippy connected, loaded the dictionary, and built the full config
# against real firmware.
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "Loaded MCU 'mcu'"
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "Configured MCU 'mcu'"
# Positive proof the HC32 ADC stub delivered samples: both thermistors
# read ~36 C, so a periodic Stats line carries "temp=36.x" / "temp=3x".
# A broken ADC leaves them at temp=0.0, which this catches where the
# no-error assertions below cannot.
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "temp=3"
# A renode-side crash (wrong sysbus peripheral, hook callback error)
# kills serial; klippy then gets EOF and identify times out.
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Got EOF when reading from device"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Internal error"
# A broken ADC handshake (channel never completes, value out of range)
# would surface as an out-of-range shutdown rather than a clean read.
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "ADC out of range"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Timer too close"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Rescheduled timer in the past"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Klippy is shutdown"
